@flexiarg math-formalization-GN/connectedness-component-api
@title Expose Component Openness Before Reasoning About Frontiers
@keywords connected-component, locally-connected, openness, interior, frontier, counting, unbounded-exterior
@audience lean-provers, formalizers, topologists
@tone procedural
@difficulty intermediate
@cross-list [CV]
@why math-informal/local-to-global
@see-also math-formalization-GN/connected-union-via-common-point math-formalization-CV/holomorphic-disk-api

! conclusion:
  Reasoning about the components of an open set runs through their openness:
  establish it first, convert membership to interior membership, and only then
  relate component frontiers to the ambient one.

  + context: A topological obligation about `connectedComponentIn U x` for an
    open `U` in a locally connected space — counting components, locating their
    frontiers, or separating bounded from unbounded ones.

  + IF:
      The space is locally connected and `U` is open, then components of `U` are
      themselves open, and that single fact is what makes the rest mechanical:
      `interior_eq` converts component membership into exactly the form frontier
      reasoning consumes, and the frontier of a component can then be placed
      inside the ambient frontier of `U`.

  + HOWEVER:
      The openness step is the one the classical argument omits, because
      classically it is obvious. Skipped, every subsequent frontier manipulation
      is off by the difference between a set and its interior, and the failure
      surfaces several lemmas later as an unprovable membership rather than as a
      missing hypothesis.

      Counting components needs a separation the topology does not supply on its
      own. The complement of a compact set has one unbounded component and
      finitely many bounded ones, and these are counted by different means:
      inject the bounded interior components into algebraic witnesses — roots, in
      the lemniscate case — and reserve a single marker for the common unbounded
      exterior. Counting them uniformly produces an off-by-one that is easy to
      misattribute to the injection.

      Do not expect the general statement to be present under the name you would
      give it. This corner of the library is thin enough that an anchor from a
      community discussion may be the best available evidence about what exists,
      and it should be recorded as such rather than treated as an API reference.

  + THEN:
      Prove component openness first, as its own step, and use `interior_eq` to
      move membership into the frontier-ready form.

      Relate the component's frontier to the ambient frontier of `U` explicitly,
      rather than reasoning about closures ad hoc.

      When counting, separate the bounded interior components from the single
      unbounded exterior one, inject the bounded family into whatever algebraic
      witnesses the problem supplies, and add the exterior back as a marker.

  + BECAUSE:
      Local connectedness is the hypothesis that makes components behave like
      pieces rather than like arbitrary closed subsets, and openness is the form
      in which the library exposes that. Almost every difficulty in this area is
      the consequence of using a component as if it were open without having
      said so — which is free in prose and unavailable to a proof assistant. The
      classical picture also silently distinguishes the unbounded exterior from
      the bounded pieces; making that distinction explicit is what turns a
      component count into an injection plus a constant.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Prove that components of the open set are open before anything else.]
    next[Use interior_eq to turn component membership into the frontier-ready form.]
    next[State the containment of the component frontier in the ambient frontier as its own lemma.]
    next[Separate the bounded components from the single unbounded exterior when counting.]
    next[Inject bounded components into the problem's algebraic witnesses and add the exterior as a marker.]
